{"artifact_reads":[{"digest_matched":true,"path":".gnd-planning/milestones/python-snapshot-evaluation/notes/python-snapshot-evaluation-plan.md"}],"concern_count":4,"disposition":"revise","reviewer_id":"glm","schema_version":2,"summary":"Digest verified (6766 bytes, sha256 1fb9b6a8...). PS1 envelope, outer-failure, input-error and vacuity controls are carried into concrete falsifiable checks via the referenced phase-PS1 spec (plain and both wrapped reports, missing file with zero fail counts, unknown schema, vacuous exit 2 vs opt-in exit 0); PS2 single-evaluation and PS3 sharing/negation-invalidation have concrete section 9 exercises. Four stated exit criteria however have no named verification step: PS4 dependency testing is contradicted by section 9's 'separate gates' disclaimer, and PS4 model invalidation, PS3 exhaustion reporting, and PS2's empty-vs-uncomputable availability distinction are named as deliverables but never exercised."}
{"concern_id":"ps4-dependency-gate-scope","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/python-snapshot-evaluation/notes/python-snapshot-evaluation-plan.md","location":"section 4, PS4 row (line 34) versus section 9 (line 60) and section 3 (line 25)","observation":"Section 4 makes dependency testing the observable PS4 exit criterion: 'Test shared dependency ranges against pinned builds and an independent host.' But section 9's PS4 scenario covers only worker lifecycle ('execute two requests in one isolated process, force one invalid input, then evaluate a valid request; log request IDs, model identities and restart behavior') and then disowns the dependency side: 'Lockfile reproducibility and shared-host installation are separate gates.' Section 3 further weakens it to 'PS4 may measure dependency compatibility independently'. No section names an owner, criterion, or location for those separate gates, and section 7's outside-milestone list does not include them, so the stated exit criterion can close unverified."}],"recommendation":"Reconcile the two halves: either add a concrete section 9 PS4 exercise for dependency compatibility (e.g. install from the pinned lockfile into a clean environment and run the two-request sequence; repeat the sequence on a host carrying the shared dependency set, asserting the recorded dependency versions), or restate section 4's PS4 exit criterion to exclude it and name where the separate gate's criterion and ruling live. Falsifiable alternative: the phase cannot be ruled closed until a pinned-build install log and a shared-host install log exist for the recorded dependency matrix.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"high","title":"PS4 exit criterion includes dependency testing that section 9 disowns as a separate gate"}
{"concern_id":"ps4-model-invalidation-unexercised","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/python-snapshot-evaluation/notes/python-snapshot-evaluation-plan.md","location":"section 2 (line 21) and section 4 PS4 row (line 34) versus section 9 (line 60)","observation":"Sections 2 and 4 both commit to 'Specify and exercise a versioned persistent local worker with repeated requests, structured errors and model invalidation', but the section 9 PS4 scenario is only 'execute two requests in one isolated process, force one invalid input, then evaluate a valid request; log request IDs, model identities and restart behavior.' No step changes or invalidates a model; logging model identities observes them without exercising invalidation, and the invalid-input step exercises structured errors, not model invalidation."}],"recommendation":"Add a section 9 step that forces invalidation, e.g. between two requests in one worker process change the model identity or its versioned stamp and assert the next request re-loads the model rather than serving stale state (falsifiable alternative: a stale-identity response fails the phase), or stop the worker mid-sequence and assert the following request recovers with a fresh identity and restart record.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"PS4's model invalidation is named to be exercised but no verification step exercises it"}
{"concern_id":"ps3-exhaustion-not-verified","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/python-snapshot-evaluation/notes/python-snapshot-evaluation-plan.md","location":"section 4 PS3 row (line 33) and section 2 (line 19) versus section 9 (line 60)","observation":"The PS3 deliverable and exit criterion both require 'Report per-target work and exhaustion', but the section 9 PS3 scenario covers only sharing and negation invalidation: 'replay two targets against one loaded snapshot and invalidate a negative witness when the blocked relation changes.' No scenario drives a per-target budget to exhaustion, in contrast to PS2 which does exercise its budget control ('Exercise a redacted role and a capped extension'); section 6's generic rule 'Record work counters and the first refusal where limits apply' names the control but attaches no PS3 case to it."}],"recommendation":"Add a section 9 PS3 step: set one target's work limit below its requirement, assert the recorded per-target counter and the first refusal for that target while the other target still completes on the same loaded snapshot (falsifiable alternative: a target silently truncated or a refusal attributed to the wrong target fails the phase).","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"PS3's per-target exhaustion reporting has no verification scenario"}
{"concern_id":"ps2-availability-distinction-untested","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/python-snapshot-evaluation/notes/python-snapshot-evaluation-plan.md","location":"section 2 (line 17) and section 4 PS2 row (line 32) versus section 9 (line 60)","observation":"Sections 2 and 4 require 'Distinguish a complete empty extension from one that could not be computed', but the section 9 PS2 checks are 'compare requested inferred rows to a hand-built extension from the #326 fixture; count evaluations, expecting one. Exercise a redacted role and a capped extension.' None of these produces an empty-but-complete extension or a not-computable one, so the availability distinction in the exit criterion is never observed, and no PS2 spec exists yet to carry it ('Later specs follow the preceding decisions')."}],"recommendation":"Add a paired section 9 PS2 check: one input whose derived extension is genuinely empty (assert zero rows with the availability marker indicating success) and one where computation cannot complete (assert the same request reports not-computed rather than an empty success), which also directly instantiates the section 0 rule that a failure must never look like an empty successful result.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"low","title":"PS2's empty-versus-uncomputable availability distinction lacks any exercise"}
